export abstract anchor FactoryAdoption:
    projectSpecificRealization:
        This project's primary deliverable is the factory specification, not a
        single mandated factory implementation. Consuming projects tether the specification
        through Piton and use it to build a factory suited to their project.
        The engineer chooses the implementation language and other appropriate
        implementation choices. Each project's factory grows gradually with
        that project's needs. Keep reusable requirements distinct from those
        project-specific implementation choices. Do not assume one shared
        factory service or centrally shared model-performance history.
    referenceImplementation:
        The reference implementation is optional and lives in a separate
        repository. It is a worked example, not a dependency, a required
        template, or a fork source. The package declares the baseline stack. A
        reference implementation may claim conformance only through a
        conformance report owned by FactoryVerification and an independent
        verifier; otherwise it is only an example. It declares a supported specification range, and any
        specification-version change expires its conformance report. Drift is
        permitted but visible. Scaffold agents may not inspect or copy it
        without explicit engineer authorization. Tooling work is not gated on
        it, and its absence never blocks onboarding, adoption, or conformance.
    adoptionMapping:
        Every imported record passes an admission rule with a disposition:
        retained, translated, reassessed, or discarded, with a recorded
        rationale and engineer approval for discard or reassessment. Provenance
        is required for every record: source location, source version, content
        hash, source factory identity, tether and configuration versions, and
        mapping.
        Retain or translate only when hash verification, ingestion policy,
        secret quarantine, and pinned ref or durable diff all pass; otherwise
        reassess. Imported plans must be closed over their dependencies and
        evidence; incomplete plans are parked non-dispatchable. Completed work
        is evidence and produces zero launches and zero effects.
        A foreign live session gets a foreign-session record with harness,
        reference, old owner, lease and epoch or quarantine, and an engineer
        decision to adopt, stop, or abandon. Adoption mints a new lease and
        epoch under FactorySessions and requires evidence that the old owner
        stopped dispatching or is unreachable. Unknown liveness and unsupported
        discovery keep the session quarantined.
        The first milestone is one imported, still-relevant pending order
        reaching recorded final acceptance with required evidence, plus one
        mid-order restart with no duplicate effects. The import report records
        who judged relevance.
    adoptionConformance:
        Adoption conformance cells have expected observations and path-reached
        assertions. They cover: disposition and provenance for every imported
        record; a completed imported result with zero launches and zero effects
        under a positive control that the observation path records a launch when
        one is expected; a live foreign session with an ownership decision and
        no duplicate; one imported order reaching final acceptance with lineage
        to its imported identity and source version; every other imported order
        fully mapped or parked non-dispatchable with zero launches; an uncertain
        or unverified completion not trusted as done; crash-mid-import
        resumability with no duplicate records and no half-imported dispatchable
        plan; a mid-order restart evaluated by the recovery matrix exactly once;
        and reference implementation absent, with import and resumption still
        passing, and present, with no change to scaffold, descriptor, onboarding,
        routing, or verdicts. Counts alone never pass a cell.
    incrementalAdoptionAndEvolution:
        A consuming project initially implements only the factory capabilities
        it needs. As needs grow, its factory can read the tethered factory
        specification and use its own task execution capabilities to dispatch
        work that enhances its implementation. Support this self-evolution
        rather than requiring every specified capability at initial adoption.
        Enhancement needs are identified both when work is blocked or a
        reusable improvement is noticed during assigned work and through
        periodic review of completed work, recurring blockers, repeated manual
        steps, and resource data. Both paths feed the same enhancement approval
        policy. Review scheduling, proposals, approval, routing evidence, and
        category lifecycle follow FactoryEvolution. Activation follows the
        self-update continuity requirements.
    selfEnhancementAuthority:
        Ask the engineer before initiating factory enhancement tasks by
        default. The engineer may authorize automatic approval of future
        enhancements within the scope of that instruction. Such authorization
        does not itself expand account budgets or other configured limits.
    needDrivenEnhancement:
        Prefer fulfilling an order with existing capabilities when they can
        do the work well. Propose a factory enhancement when a missing
        capability blocks the order or would materially improve delivery,
        especially when it will be reused. Tie self-improvement to project
        needs and follow the established enhancement approval policy.
    selfUpdateContinuity:
        Factory enhancements must preserve ongoing work. The update mechanism
        depends on the technology chosen by the consuming project. Apply
        updates while running when the implementation supports doing so
        without stopping ongoing work. Otherwise wait for a suitable moment
        to restart, or propose a restart to the engineer. Do not mandate one
        universal update mechanism across implementations.
    tetherUpdateReconciliation:
        When an update to the tethered factory specification conflicts with
        project-specific customizations, an agent proposes a reconciliation
        for the engineer to decide. Do not silently give either the updated
        specification or existing customizations precedence.
    specificationChangeAuthority:
        When agents discover a gap or contradiction requiring a project
        specification change, they ask the engineer before amending it by
        default. The engineer may grant broad authorization, such as go ahead
        and make all modifications, allowing agents to make specification
        changes within that authorization without seeking approval for each
        change. The scope and duration of that authorization follow the
        engineer's instruction.
    configuration:
        Initial configuration is developed through a series of prompt
        questions driven by an agent. The resulting configuration is written to
        the implementation's actual configuration file and recorded through the
        descriptor. Onboarding must always establish the implementation
        choice, available harnesses and account kinds, budgets and allowance
        limits, build and test capacity, delivery preference, and an approved
        initial operating policy. It asks further questions only for the chosen
        capabilities, remote-build setup, routing thresholds, and existing-work
        import. File format and location remain project-specific. Onboarding
        states, outputs, the decision set, the credential boundary, change
        application, and acceptance follow FactoryOnboarding.
    initialOperatingPolicy:
        During onboarding, an agent recommends an initial operating policy
        based on the project's needs, available accounts, and build capacity.
        The engineer reviews and approves that policy. The resource controller
        may tune execution within the approved policy; this does not authorize
        changing its boundaries or increasing budgets without approval.
    existingWorkResumption:
        Support continuing work begun under the existing ad hoc factory rather
        than requiring that work to start over. Represent the project context,
        workflow, and relevant prior progress through the new factory's general
        concepts. The mechanism for carrying over existing artifacts and
        reconciling them with the new factory remains undecided; this does not
        require reproducing the old factory's implementation or fixed pipeline.
        Resumption need not preserve a one-to-one mapping to the old factory.
        Many specifics may be discarded. Inspect the existing factory to inform
        further interview questions about what to include and disregard, rather
        than treating its details as requirements by default.
    evidenceBasedAdoption:
        As a general capability, assess whatever evidence already exists of a
        project's build process, factory, or ad hoc pseudo-factory. Import its
        data and work while transitioning it to the new project-specific
        factory. This applies beyond Popcorn and includes assessment of the
        evidence rather than automatically trusting every existing status or
        requiring all work to start over. The import mechanism and exact
        mapping of existing artifacts remain project-specific decisions.
